<title>MALAYSIA: Malaysia 1997 c/a deficit seen lower - Reuter poll [CORRECTED 09:40 GMT].</title>
<headline>Malaysia 1997 c/a deficit seen lower - Reuter poll [CORRECTED 09:40 GMT].</headline>
<dateline>KUALA LUMPUR 1996-12-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>(Corrects JP Morgan's figures in table from dollars which produced false figures).</p>
<p>Fast-growing Malaysia's current account deficit, which sparked fears of overheating last year, is expected to dip sharply in the current year and remain around those levels next year, analysts said.</p>
<p>In a poll by Reuters, 16 research houses in Malaysia and Singapore predicted the current account deficit in 1997 would be an average 13.03 billion ringgit</p>
<p>(Corrects from an average 12.40 billion ringgit) against the government's forecast of 11.50 billion.</p>
<p>They also forecast an average current account gap of 13.67 billion ringgit</p>
<p>(Corrects from 12.35 billion ringgit)</p>
<p>in 1996 against the government's 14.77 billion.</p>
<p>In 1995, Malaysia reported a current account deficit of 18.69 billion ringgit.  </p>
